<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/storm-front.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/storm-front-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/storm-front.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="658" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.15"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/property-claim-appraisal/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Wind &amp; Microburst Claim Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Wind Damage Appraisal After Idaho Windstorms and Microbursts</h1><p class="lede">Idaho’s wind comes in bursts: a thunderstorm microburst over Nampa, a canyon gust in Pocatello, a regional windstorm that drops pines across Coeur d’Alene. We value what it took apart.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-y" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-w" href="/schedule-a-consult/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="facts"><div><dt>Common causes</dt><dd>Microbursts, windstorms</dd></div><div><dt>Damage</dt><dd>Roofs, siding, trees</dd></div><div><dt>Key issue</dt><dd>Wind vs. wear</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>How wind shows up on Idaho claims</h2>
<p>The open Snake River Plain lets wind accelerate with little in the way. Pocatello and the Portneuf Gap, the Twin Falls rim and the stretch between Mountain Home and Glenns Ferry are known for strong gusts. Summer thunderstorms add microbursts: short, violent downdrafts that can flatten fences and peel shingles in a narrow path. In North Idaho, the November 2015 windstorm brought down thousands of trees across Kootenai County and the surrounding region, a reminder of how much damage falling timber can do.</p>
<h2>Where wind damage appraisals get contested</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Creased and unsealed shingles</strong> that look intact but have lost their bond, which carriers sometimes call wear and tear.</li>
<li><strong>Siding and soffit</strong> where only the torn panels are priced and the matching pieces on the same elevation are ignored.</li>
<li><strong>Tree removal limits</strong> applied to complex jobs that need cranes, and debris removal left out.</li>
<li><strong>Fence and outbuilding claims</strong> under Other Structures coverage with low limits.</li>
<li><strong>Interior water damage</strong> from wind-driven rain after an opening was created.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Building the record</h2>
<p>Wind claims turn on causation and scope. We document direction of damage, lifted tabs, nail-pull patterns and debris fields, and compare them with recorded gust data for your area and date. Then we price the repair to restore a uniform, weather-tight roof or wall, not a patchwork.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Tornadoes happen too</b>Idaho sees a small number of tornadoes most years, usually brief and weak, mostly on the Snake River Plain. The appraisal approach is the same: document, measure and price the full repair.</div>

<h2>Questions about wind damage appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>The adjuster says my shingles were already failing. What now?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Ask for the reasoning in writing. An independent inspection can show whether damage follows a wind pattern or is consistent with age. If coverage is accepted and only scope is disputed, appraisal may help.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is tree removal covered if the tree didn’t hit anything?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Often only in limited situations, and usually with a low per-tree cap. When a tree damages a covered structure, removal is commonly included within the policy’s limits.</p></div></details><details><summary>How soon should I tarp a wind-damaged roof?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>As soon as it’s safe. Policies require reasonable steps to prevent further damage, and those costs are usually reimbursable.</p></div></details></div>
